nodecode-ghost-call is a rewrite of winchxyz's winchxyz/hermes-ghost-call:

  https://github.com/winchxyz/hermes-ghost-call
  commit b670ff8a930a02aff8afe2c39994bcf4645654ac
  MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 winchxyz (LICENSE)

The plugin was seven Hermes tools, a /ghost slash command, a skill, and a
FastAPI bridge that carried a live phone call: two browser pages relayed
mu-law audio, OpenAI realtime transcription (plus a text model for hints and
TTS for the earpiece) ran in the bridge, and Twilio legs could put the call
through the operator's own account. The hint side of that bridge is what this
rewrite keeps, in Common Lisp: the cheap gate that drops fillers, greetings,
backchannels and repeats before any model call, the hint prompt with its one
line, one action, SILENT-otherwise contract and its private-target rule, the
four-second floor and the forty-hint cap, the cleaning of an answer that came
back as a question or a repeat, the intervene gate the operator opens by hand
with the AI disclosure on the first line, and the single post-call summary
(agreed, date, amount, open items, draft follow-up) with its fallback when
the model cannot answer. The seven tools keep their names and jobs, except
that the lines of the call arrive as text instead of a microphone, so
ghost_line is where the operator or the model hands the coach what was just
said, and ghost_speak (the earpiece whisper) is gone because a cell has
nothing to play audio on. What was left out, and why: the audio bridge,
the browser pages, the realtime transcription, the earpiece TTS, the Twilio
mode and its dial allowlist, the SQLite store shared by two processes (one
process serves this rewrite, so the call is a struct in memory), the daily
session limit, and the per-call transcript commands — none of them survive
without a bridge and a phone line, and all of the coaching rules they served
do. Nothing of the original is vendored.
